  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the curve $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/edcf0c5993499de5157b2cca23c31789ca163e9a163d56bd8c9831fa0844b7b6.png)\n\n- To plot $y={e}^{x+2},$ we shift the graph of $y={e}^{x}$ horizontally by $2$ units to the left. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-2,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/555340d1dc2040dec951491b389d22007ec63827c2f3fb9f00748522d317845b.png)\n\n- To plot the graph $y={e}^{x+2}+1,$ we shift the graph of $y={e}^{x+2}$ vertically by $1$ unit up. The point $(-2,1)$ moves to $(-2,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/d20b4aac92e5e56aa0c9aea9e91b542e96dd23be0c303f455301c645336647fa.png)",
